Z||000000||88formula||09-29-2000||12:08 AM||||What does the fiero do for lateral g's in a 200' circle?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ed||09-29-2000||06:19 AM||||Recommended reading: "Pontiac Fiero 1984-1988."  Brooklands Books, No date.<P>This is a compilation of magazine articles.  I've casually looked through it to answer your question.  <P>Car and Driver Feb. 1986.  Fiero GT 86 1/2.  Lateral G's in 300' skidpad: 0.80 g.<P>Road and Track No date.  GT 86 1/2.  100' skidpad 0.83 g. <P>Road and Track Oct 1987  Formula.  Skidpad not specified.  0.83 g.  <P>Road and Track Sept. 1983 SE. 100' skidpad  0.812 g. <P>Car and Driver Sept. 1983  "Pilot-built car"<BR> 282' skidpad 0.81 g.  <P>Its interesting to me that both the four and six had the same lateral acceleration.  I feel the four is better handling because of it's lighter weight, typical of lighter cars. <P>What has been the experience of you auto-crossers?    ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||09-29-2000||09:55 AM||||Ed,<P> The 4 and the 6 do feel very different, but I have to side with my GT in the handling department.  I am not sure what it is, but the car has a much more neutral feel to it, and just handles more pleasantly.  I also like the fact that the GT has enough power to steer with the throttle in higher speed corners.  <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||09-29-2000||04:15 PM||||Thanks guys but I was hoping someone has the data for the 200' skidpad because this is how most cars are rated now. It gives me a way to compare the fiero to other more modern cars.<P>Thanks||||reg||1
